  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-2,$ and ${x}_{4}=5$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$ Determine the **expanded form** of $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,40$",
  "worked_solution": "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=-2,$ and ${x}_{4}=5,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =1(x-(-2))(x-(-2))(x-(-2))(x-5) \\\\  & =(x+2)(x+2)(x+2)(x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x+2)(x+2)(x-5) & =({x}^{2}+4x+4)({x}^{2}-3x-10) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-10{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-40x+4{x}^{2}-12x-40 \\\\  & ={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}-52x-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}-52x-40.$",
